merge sort is a divide-and-conquer algorithm: it recursively splits the input array in half until you're down to single elements, then merges those sorted pieces back together in order.

the real benefits over bubble sort:

1. it's a stable sort - elements with equal keys keep their relative order.
2. it runs in O(n log n) time, which stays efficient even for large inputs. bubble sort's O(n²), which gets brutal fast as input size grows.
3. it's generic - it'll sort any type of data as long as you've got a comparison function for it.

here's a rust implementation:

```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
}
